# Simple OS – hardware & VMs
# Firmware: a fixed list instead of live-build's automatic selection (tools/lb_config.sh: --firmware-chroot
# false). The automatic selection took every Bookworm package with files under /lib/firmware – including Raspberry Pi
# boot firmware, astronomy cameras, telephony cards and firmware-realtek-rtl8723cs-bt, which conflicts with
# firmware-realtek from backports (build abort). Kernel, firmware and microcode come from bookworm-backports
# (config/archives/backports.pref.chroot): kernel 6.12 knows newer graphics (e.g. Intel Raptor Lake 0xA7AA in the
# Core 5 210H – with 6.1 the screen stayed at 800x600), and firmware 2025 matches it. There
# firmware-misc-nonfree is split up (Intel graphics, MediaTek, Cirrus, NVIDIA …) – the parts are listed below.
# firmware-linux only pulls the free part without Recommends -> nonfree explicitly
firmware-linux
firmware-linux-free
firmware-linux-nonfree
firmware-misc-nonfree
# Graphics: Intel from Gen 12 (Tiger/Alder/Raptor/Meteor Lake: GuC/HuC/DMC), AMD, NVIDIA (nouveau + GSP)
firmware-intel-graphics
firmware-amd-graphics
firmware-nvidia-graphics
firmware-nvidia-gsp
# Other Intel (sensor hub/ISH, VPU …), audio (SOF: speakers/microphones of modern laptops, Cirrus amplifiers)
firmware-intel-misc
firmware-intel-sound
firmware-sof-signed
firmware-cirrus
alsa-topology-conf
# Wi-Fi/Bluetooth
firmware-iwlwifi
firmware-realtek
firmware-atheros
firmware-ath9k-htc
firmware-mediatek
firmware-brcm80211
firmware-libertas
firmware-ti-connectivity
firmware-zd1211
atmel-firmware
bluez-firmware
wireless-regdb
# Wired network (Broadcom NetXtreme in workstations, Realtek see above)
firmware-bnx2
firmware-bnx2x
# CPU microcode (security and stability fixes)
intel-microcode
amd64-microcode
# QEMU/SPICE: resolution + shared clipboard, guest agent
spice-vdagent
qemu-guest-agent
